{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>An ox or a sheep or a goat, when it is born.</b> The commentators said that on the day of its birth it already bears the name ox or sheep, for it adds nothing to its completeness and to its essence — for it is born with all of its completeness. This is to exclude man: עַיִר פֶּרֶא יִוָּלֵד (Job 11:12), and the attainment of his completeness depends on his deeds. And it appears that this is why Scripture made it known to us — lest you say: since on the day of their birth they already have all the completeness appropriate to them, if so they should be fit for sacrifice immediately — Scripture comes to teach: ״וְהָיָה שִׁבְעַת יָמִים תַּחַת אִמּוֹ״. ״וּמִיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי וָהָלְאָה יֵרָצֶה״, for everything of the number eight is holiness, as was explained above in parashat Shemini (Leviticus 9:1); therefore it is not fit to become holy until this number has passed over it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:54.711506219Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}